From 24f863ad061e951c0825495e7194f75aa06344e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shanks <shanks@book>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 12:12:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 19/37] feat(transportd): carry whitelisted official headers
 into the upstream projection

R-CTD (rust_sidecar) previously rebuilt every upstream request from a blank
HeaderMap, so the official `x-codex-*` metadata that the Go gateway had
already scoped per account never reached the upstream. R-Go (native direct)
does send them, so the two transports diverged.

Add the single sanctioned header channel to the cross-language contract:
`X-CTD-Carried-Headers` carries a JSON object<string,string> whose keys must
be in CARRIED_HEADER_NAMES (verbatim equal to the Go-side
codexCarriedHeaderNames). Credential keys (authorization /
chatgpt-account-id / cookie) fail closed at parse time; non-whitelisted keys
are dropped, so the carrier can never degenerate into a raw header map.

Injection points:
- responses HTTP/SSE: union with the local official projection (carried wins);
- compact: same channel;
- responses body: `client_metadata["x-codex-turn-metadata"]` is restored from
  the carried value, because the snapshot-derived projection always reports
  has_turn_metadata() == false and Go strips the client's reserved copy.

WS handshake intentionally not injected: the official WS handshake header set
is protocol-owned and never carries turn-metadata as a header
(core/tests/suite/client_websockets.rs).

Local required headers (host/content-length/accept/content-type) and the
official auth pair (authorization / chatgpt-account-id) are still written by
the official client and BearerAuthProvider.

Verified: cargo check -p codex-transportd --all-targets; cargo test
-p codex-transportd --lib (149 passed).

(cherry picked from commit ee93455495e3bc9f63aca478efff22b3353fc68b)
